// Fourth face of 00's class — the argument TYPE, and the one with a
// language-level consequence: a fused `println` renders an ABSTRACT
// type through its EXPANDED private representation.
//
//   timeout 25 graphix --no-fusion 03_abstract_repr_leak.gx
//     ["Cons", 1, "Nil"]        <- naked Value form (the abstract type
//                                  has no structural view outside its
//                                  module, so the printer falls back)
//   timeout 25 graphix 03_abstract_repr_leak.gx
//     `Cons(1, `Nil)            <- the module's PRIVATE variant shape
//
// `Print::update` renders with `TVal { env, typ: &from[1].typ(), v }`
// (graphix-package-core/src/lib.rs ~2175) — the ARGUMENT NODE's type. In
// a kernel `from[1]` is the synthetic `Ref` that `DynCallSlot::new`
// (fusion/kernel.rs ~182) built from the slot's FROZEN ABI type, and
// freezing resolves abstracts to their concrete bodies
// (`resolve_abstract`/`freeze_for_abi`). So the printer is handed the
// expansion that `type List;` exists to hide.
//
// Ordinary shapes agree — tuple, struct, variant, narrow int, nullable
// all render identically in both modes (checked), and so does the same
// value through string INTERPOLATION (`"[list::from_array([1])]"`),
// which reads the source node's type rather than a DynCall arg's. Only
// a direct call of a rendering builtin on an abstract-typed value
// diverges.
//
// NOT ORACLE-VISIBLE (stdout only; the traced value here is 0).
//
// Fix: the synthetic arg Refs must carry the call site's DECLARED
// argument types, not the ABI-frozen ones. The frozen type is a
// marshalling detail — it belongs to the CLIF signature, not to the
// builtin's view of its arguments. (This is the same repair surface as
// 00/01/02: build `DynCallSlot`'s arg Refs from the emitted argument
// nodes' spec + declared type, and typecheck the Apply.)
{ println(list::from_array([i64:1])); i64:0 }
